= WiiLand Plan =

Status: active migration plan for distributions where Wayland is the primary
session and Xorg input drivers are legacy compatibility only.

== Decision ==

The default Wayland path is a Linux uinput bridge, shipped as wiilandd and
exposed explicitly as backend=uinput/--backend uinput. It consumes
hid-wiimote/libxwiimote compatibility-library events and creates virtual evdev
devices. Wayland compositors already ingest those devices through libinput and
native game stacks already ingest them through evdev, SDL, Wine, and Proton.

This is intentionally not an XWayland or Xorg input-driver port. Wayland clients
cannot generally inject input into other clients. Compositor-specific virtual
input protocols are not portable enough for the default backend. libei is the
preferred future compositor-mediated backend once it is broadly available; it
will be an optional backend, not a replacement for the uinput baseline.

== Current shipped behavior ==

wiilandd:
  - monitors hid-wiimote devices through libxwiimote compatibility library;
  - creates one virtual uinput gamepad per Wii Remote by default;
  - optionally creates a virtual desktop mouse/keyboard with --profile desktop
    or --profile both;
  - forwards core, Nunchuk, Classic Controller, Pro Controller, Guitar, and
    Drums button events to the gamepad profile;
  - forwards accelerometer, MotionPlus, Nunchuk, Classic Controller,
    Pro Controller, Guitar, Drums, and Balance Board axes to the gamepad
    profile;
  - maps Wii Remote core input in the desktop profile: D-pad repeat and valid
    IR events move the pointer, A/B click, +/- send Enter/Escape, Home sends
    the compositor overview key, and 1/2 send Page Down/Page Up;
  - supports key=value configuration for default profile, syspath and devtype
    profile overrides, desktop button actions, D-pad pointer speed, IR pointer
    gain, IR jitter deadzone, IR smoothing, IR tracker selection, optional IR
    screen calibration, modern motion-aim output, and persistent flat-surface
    aim calibration through /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf,
    ~/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf, or --config; res/wiilandd.conf is the
    distributable sample;
  - exposes first-class motion aiming for Steam and non-Steam games:
    aim-mode=right-stick maps the selected sensor to ABS_RX/ABS_RY camera aim,
    aim-mode=mouse maps it to REL_X/REL_Y pointer aim, aim-source=auto prefers
    IR when visible, then calibrated MotionPlus, then calibrated accelerometer
    tilt, --calibrate-aim emits flat-surface offsets, and IR can stay relative
    or use optional screen bounds for absolute positional aim;
  - provides --self-test for deterministic profile, button-map, config, and
    pointer-motion validation without hardware;
  - provides --axis-map, --validation-checklist, and --doctor for
    machine-readable virtual gamepad mapping, required hardware-validation
    coverage, and runtime readiness diagnostics;
  - ships CI and make-check smoke coverage that compiles wiilandd against
    committed libxwiimote stubs and runs --self-test, --check-config,
    --dump-config, --axis-map, --validation-checklist, and --doctor;
    CI also builds the Autotools package and validates the staged udev,
    systemd-user, example-config, and wiilandd binary install paths;
  - provides --dry-run --trace-events for hardware validation reports without
    creating virtual input devices;
  - handles hotplug and extension changes without Xorg configuration.

Required runtime permissions:
  - normal input read access for hid-wiimote evdev nodes;
  - write access to /dev/uinput, normally via a distribution udev rule or a
    service running with the input/uinput capability policy. The sample service
    validates configuration with --check-config before starting.

== Roadmap ==

1. Modern motion aiming
   Shipped as aim-mode=right-stick or aim-mode=mouse with aim-source=auto
   fallback from IR to MotionPlus to accelerometer, robust dual-source IR
   midpoint tracking with optional absolute screen bounds, configurable
   hold-to-aim activation, sensitivity, deadzone, smoothing, axis inversion, and
   Wii-style flat-surface calibration for accelerometer zero and MotionPlus gyro
   bias.
   This makes Wii Remote hardware useful as a modern Linux game controller in
   Steam, Proton/Wine, SDL, XWayland games, and native Wayland games without
   spoofing a DualSense/DualShock device identity.

2. Desktop profile
   Shipped as --profile desktop and --profile both with D-pad motion,
   configurable IR pointer motion, jitter deadzone, smoothing, and configurable
   desktop button actions.

3. Per-device policy
   Shipped as device.<syspath-substring>.profile and
   device-type.<devtype-substring>.profile rules for choosing gamepad, desktop,
   or both per controller path/type. Bluetooth-address matching remains a future
   refinement if hardware reports show a stable identifier surface worth
   standardizing.

4. Optional libei backend
   Add --backend=libei when libei and compositor support are common enough.
   libei gives user-mediated emulated input through a compositor EIS endpoint;
   uinput remains the fallback because it works across compositors and native
   game stacks today.

5. Distribution integration
   Automake installs the uinput udev rule, graphical-session-scoped systemd
   user service, and sample config from res/. Keep the legacy Xorg blacklist
   file for legacy Xorg sessions only.

6. Test hardware matrix
   Completion requires direct reports for:
   - original Wii Remote: core buttons, accelerometer, IR desktop pointer;
   - original Wii Remote plus external MotionPlus addon: hotplug detection and
     MotionPlus X/Y/Z axes;
   - Wii Remote Plus / built-in MotionPlus: core buttons, accelerometer,
     MotionPlus X/Y/Z axes;
   - Nunchuk: stick, C/Z buttons, accelerometer passthrough;
   - Classic Controller: sticks, triggers, face buttons;
   - Wii U Pro Controller: sticks, triggers, face buttons;
   - Guitar: fret buttons, strum bar, whammy, stick;
   - Drums: pad hits, cymbal/tom pressure axes, bass and hi-hat pedals;
   - Balance Board: all four weight sensors.

   Each report must record kernel version, libxwiimote version, wiilandd
   --version output, git commit, compositor, distribution, Bluetooth adapter,
   device devtype (for example gen10/gen15/gen20), extension state, and whether
   SDL, Wine/Proton, and native Wayland desktop profile behavior matched
   expectations.

   Recommended capture sequence:
     tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh
     tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh N
     tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh N --trace-events=motion-plus
     tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh N --trace-events=ir

   The first command records finite report schema version, host, UTC capture
   timestamp, distribution, compositor/session environment, Bluetooth
   controller, hid-wiimote module-parameter, permission, config, version, git
   commit/dirty state, axis map, validation checklist, doctor diagnostics,
   device-list, devtype, extension, sysfs uevent identity, battery, event-node
   access diagnostics, and manual validation placeholders for SDL, Wine/Proton,
   and native Wayland desktop behavior.
   The second command captures sequenced, filtered --dry-run --trace-events
   --verbose output for one device. Extra arguments are passed to wiilandd; use
   --trace-events=motion-plus when validating MotionPlus only,
   --trace-events=ir when tuning IR pointer input, or use the number from
   --list or a sysfs path.

   After dry-run traces show expected events, repeat without --dry-run and
   validate the virtual gamepad in an SDL input tester, one Steam Input game,
   one non-Steam native or XWayland game, one Wine/Proton game, aim-mode=mouse,
   aim-mode=right-stick, and the desktop pointer/buttons under a native Wayland
   compositor. Do not mark the Wayland migration complete until this matrix has
   real hardware evidence.
